<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :theme_typefaces %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:theme_typefaces) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Set the Font') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Declare your font with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">@font-face</code>,
            then include the typeface mixin in your theme. Framework 3.4 or later.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.typeface($family)</code> sets the font of every text class to
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$family</code>, with Inter Variable and Inter as fallbacks.
              Pass one quoted family name, or a comma-separated list for your own fallbacks.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@use "framework/config/layers";
@use "framework/mixins/theme-slots";

@include layers.order;

@font-face {
    font-family: "Example Sans";
    font-weight: 100 900;
    font-style: normal;
    src: url("./fonts/example-sans.woff2") format("woff2");
}

@layer tn--themes {
    .trmnl .screen--theme-example {
        @include theme-slots.typeface("Example Sans");
    }
}',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Load the Theme') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Compile the example to <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">example.css</code> and load it next to the framework stylesheet.
            Add the theme class to the screen, and its text switches to your font. The two stylesheets can load in either order.
          </p>
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/3.4.0/plugins.min.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/themes/example.css">
<body class="trmnl">
  <div class="screen screen--theme-example">
    <div class="view view--full">
      <div class="layout"><span class="title">Today at a glance</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</body>', language: 'html') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'What Changes on Each Device') %>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Every text class uses your font: titles, labels, values, descriptions, the title bar, item indexes, and rich text.</li>
            <li>On 1-bit and other low-density screens, your font also replaces the Classic and TRMNL pixel fonts.</li>
            <li>Text sizes, line heights, and Text Scale do not change. On 1-bit and other low-density screens they are the sizes drawn for the pixel fonts.</li>
            <li>A font wider or taller than the built-in ones can wrap or clip differently at those sizes, so preview it on the devices you target.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.font-weight-shift</code> still moves every weight, as long as your font has the weights to move to.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :font_family) %> for the built-in fonts and when each device uses them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Font Files') %>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>The text classes ask for weights from 200 to 700. A variable font that covers that range matches every one of them.</li>
            <li>With separate files per weight, the browser uses the closest weight you declare. Ship at least a regular (400) and a bold (700).</li>
            <li>Host the files beside the stylesheet and point <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">src</code> at them with relative URLs, so the theme works on any host.</li>
            <li>Include the font's license with the files, and use WOFF2 where you can.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Fonts in JavaScript') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.type()</code> returns your font for any text role, so text you draw yourself matches the theme.
            Read it again after a theme switch, and wait for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">document.fonts.ready</code> before measuring text.
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_typography) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Share a Theme With Its Font') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme with a custom font ships as a folder: the compiled stylesheet with the font files and their license beside it.
            See the portable exports on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
